prompt:
You are evaluating a source-code privacy technique. Your job is to defeat it.

A tool called `effigy` takes a source file from some **original problem domain** and rewrites or
obscures it so the file can be sent to a third-party model for architectural review without
revealing what the original code was about. You are that third party, and you are hostile.

You have **complete knowledge of the tool**. It is a public research repository: its algorithms,
its configuration, and its prompts are all published. Assume nothing is secret except the original
source itself. Specifically:

Solution `llm-translation` — whole-file rewrite into a different problem domain by a language model.

A local or hosted model is given the file and instructed to rewrite it into a target domain. The
system prompt is published; its instructions are, in summary: rename every module, package, class,
function, method, variable, parameter, field and constant; rewrite every string literal, comment
and docstring into the target domain; replace every acronym, data literal, proper noun, URL, file
path, table and column name; but **preserve the architecture exactly** — same control flow, same
function count and arity, same class hierarchy, same coupling, same dead code, same god-objects,
same dependency cycles, same comment positions and speech acts, same naming morphology, same
length. It is explicitly told not to fix bugs, remove duplication, or improve the code, because the
defects are the object of study. The only names it is told to leave alone are those the language
owns: keywords, builtins, standard-library symbols, and public third-party package APIs.

The target domain is selected from a **fixed public catalog of nine entries**, and for a given run
one target is pinned for the whole file. The complete catalog is:

  graph-traversal, tensor-pipeline, event-scheduler, chess-engine, recipe-manager,
  wildlife-tracker, logistics-dispatch, music-streaming, hotel-booking

**So if the artifact reads as one of those nine, that is almost certainly the TARGET the tool chose,
not the source you are being asked for.** Known weaknesses you may exploit: the rewrite is
prompt-enforced with no verifier, it is not byte-stable, and it has been observed to leave numeric
literals, and sometimes whole spans of the original file, unrewritten.

**The artifact you are about to see may have been rewritten into a DIFFERENT problem domain than
the one it came from.** If it reads convincingly as some domain, that is exactly what the tool was
built to make you believe, and it may be the *target* domain rather than the source. Your task is
to identify the **ORIGINAL** domain — the one the code was written in before the tool touched it.


## The menu

Exactly one of these twelve is the original domain. Some of the others are domains this tool
rewrites *into*, so they are deliberately tempting.

1. Municipal water metering — Service points, meter reads, and consumption billing for a municipal water utility.
2. Wildlife tracking — Tagged animal sightings, collar telemetry, and habitat surveys for a field research program.
3. Seismic event monitoring — Seismograph station networks, waveform ingest, and earthquake event cataloging.
4. Aircraft airworthiness tracking — Component life limits, maintenance intervals, and airworthiness directive compliance.
5. Amateur radio contact logging — Logging two-way radio contacts, confirmations, and award tracking for amateur radio operators.
6. Textile mill production — Loom scheduling, yarn lot tracking, and shift production reporting in a weaving mill.
7. Hotel reservations — Room inventory, availability search, and guest reservations for a hotel chain.
8. Recipe management — Recipes, ingredients, and meal planning for a cooking application.
9. Music streaming — Track catalogs, playlists, and listener session handling for a streaming service.
10. Library catalog and circulation — Bibliographic holdings, branch lending, reservations, and returns for a public library system.
11. Event scheduling — Venues, sessions, and attendee registration for a conference scheduling system.
12. Freight dispatch — Shipment routing, carrier assignment, and delivery tracking for a freight operation.

## The artifact

One source file.

```tsx
import { useState } from "scheduler";
import {
  TaskError,
  scheduleTask,
  fetchTaskList,
  type TaskPatch,
  type TaskDetails,
  type TaskSummary,
} from "./task/api";
import { ExceptionForm } from "./components/ExceptionForm";
import { TaskDetail } from "./components/TaskDetail";

export function EventLoopApp() {
  const [routeCode, setRouteCode] = useState("");
  const [cycleCode, setCycleCode] = useState("");
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState<TaskSummary[]>([]);
  const [totalCount, setTotalCount] = useState<number | null>(null);
  const [selectedTask, setSelectedTask] = useState<TaskDetails | null>(null);
  const [flagging, setFlagging] = useState(false);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [saving, setSaving] = useState(false);
  const [errorMessage, setErrorMessage] = useState<string | null>(null);

  const describeError = (err: unknown): string =>
    err instanceof TaskError ? `${err.code}: ${err.message}` : "the task could not be completed";

  const executeSearch = async () => {
    setLoading(true);
    setErrorMessage(null);
    setSelectedTask(null);
    setFlagging(false);
    try {
      const result = await fetchTaskList(routeCode, { cycleCode: cycleCode || undefined, limit: 50 });
      setTasks(result.tasks);
      setTotalCount(result.totalCount);
    } catch (err) {
      setTasks([]);
      setTotalCount(null);
      setErrorMessage(describeError(err));
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  };

  const loadTask = async (taskId: string) => {
    setLoading(true);
    setErrorMessage(null);
    setFlagging(false);
    try {
      setSelectedTask(await fetchTask(taskId));
    } catch (err) {
      setSelectedTask(null);
      setErrorMessage(describeError(err));
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  };

  const applyException = async (patch: TaskPatch) => {
    if (selectedTask === null) {
      return;
    }
    setSaving(true);
    setErrorMessage(null);
    try {
      setSelectedTask(await scheduleTask(selectedTask.taskId, patch));
      setFlagging(false);
    } catch (err) {
      setErrorMessage(describeError(err));
    } finally {
      setSaving(false);
    }
  };

  const clearSearch = () => {
    setRouteCode("");
    setCycleCode("");
    setTasks([]);
    setTotalCount(null);
    setSelectedTask(null);
    setFlagging(false);
    setErrorMessage(null);
  };

  return (
    <div className="app-shell">
      <header className="app-header">
        <div>
          <h1>EventWorks</h1>
          <p>Citywide Scheduler Console</p>
        </div>
      </header>

      {errorMessage !== null && <p className="error-banner">{errorMessage}</p>}

      <main className="app-body">
        <section className="app-column-left">
          <form className="route-form" onSubmit={(e) => { e.preventDefault(); executeSearch(); }}>
            <h2>Locate tasks by route</h2>
            <label htmlFor="f-route">Route code</label>
            <input id="f-route" value={routeCode} placeholder="RT0007"
              onChange={(e) => setRouteCode(e.target.value)} />
            <label htmlFor="f-cycle">Scheduling cycle (optional)</label>
            <input id="f-cycle" value={cycleCode} placeholder="20260701"
              onChange={(e) => setCycleCode(e.target.value)} />
            <div className="search-actions">
              <button type="submit" className="btn btn-primary" disabled={routeCode.trim() === "" || loading}>
                {loading ? "Locating…" : "Locate"}
              </button>
              <button type="button" className="btn" onClick={clearSearch} disabled={loading}>
                Reset
              </button>
            </div>
          </form>
          {totalCount !== null && <p className="result-count">{totalCount} tasks located</p>}
          {tasks.length === 0 ? (
            <p className="empty-note">No tasks located for that route.</p>
          ) : (
            <table className="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Task id</th>
                  <th>Resource</th>
                  <th>Status</th>
                  <th>Priority</th>
                  <th>Due Date</th>
                  <th>Completion Status</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {tasks.map((task) => (
                  <tr key={task.taskId} onClick={() => loadTask(task.taskId)}
                    className={task.taskId === selectedTask?.taskId ? "row-selected" : ""}>
                    <td>{task.taskId}</td>
                    <td>{task.resourceId}</td>
                    <td>{task.status}</td>
                    <td>{task.priority}</td>
                    <td>{task.dueDate}</td>
                    <td>{task.completed ? "completed" : "pending"}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          )}
        </section>

        <section className="app-column-right">
          {selectedTask === null && <p className="empty-note">Choose a task to view its details.</p>}
          {selectedTask !== null && !flagging && (
            <TaskDetail task={selectedTask} onFlag={() => setFlagging(true)} />
          )}
          {selectedTask !== null && flagging && (
            <ExceptionForm
              task={selectedTask}
              saving={saving}
              onCancel={() => setFlagging(false)}
              onSave={applyException}
            />
          )}
        </section>
      </main>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Your answer

Reply with ONLY a JSON object. No prose before it, no explanation after it, no markdown fence.

{"choice": <integer 1-12>, "confidence": <float 0.0-1.0>}

`choice` is the menu number of the **ORIGINAL** domain. You must pick one — there is no
"cannot determine" and abstaining is not available. `confidence` is your honest probability that
your choice is correct; a low confidence is not penalised and is more useful than a bluff.
